# 46. Final Runtime Position

The iPhone stack now looks like:

```text
                         MASTER RUNTIME #10
                                  │
        ┌─────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────┐
        │                         │                         │
        ▼                         ▼                         ▼
     POWER #3                 NETWORK #4                SECURITY #7
        │                         │                         │
        │                         │                         │
        └─────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────┘
                                  │
                    ┌─────────────┴──────────────┐
                    ▼                            ▼
               CLOUD #16                   PUSH #17
                    │                            │
                    ▼                            ▼
               DATABASE #15                 APNs / iOS
                    │                            │
                    ▼                            ▼
               EVENT BUS #7                Notification UI
                    │                            │
                    └──────────────┬─────────────┘
                                   ▼
                                UI #6
```

The important separation is:

```text
#15 DATABASE
     = state

#16 CLOUD
     = synchronization

#17 PUSH
     = notification/event trigger

#7 EVENT BUS
     = internal application events

#6 UI
     = presentation
```

That prevents push notifications from becoming a hidden second database or from directly controlling application state.

## Current iPhone platform stack

```text
#1   Performance Runtime
#2   Memory Management
#3   Battery & Thermal
#4   Networking & Connectivity
#5   Persistent Storage
#6   UI & Rendering
#7   Security & Privacy
#8   On-device Intelligence
#9   Sensor & Hardware Abstraction
#10  Master Runtime & Diagnostics
#11  Advanced Memory Manager
#12  GPU / Metal Compute
#13  Advanced Camera & Imaging
#14  Media & Audio
#15  Database / Query Engine
#16  Cloud / iCloud
#17  Push Notifications
```

The natural next layer is **#18 — Background Execution Engine**, which should unify `BGTaskScheduler`, background refresh, processing tasks, URLSession background transfers, push-triggered work, task expiration, power/thermal policy, and the #10 master watchdog into one bounded scheduler.
::




# iPhone Background Execution Engine — Swift

## #18 Background Execution Engine

### Core principle

The background runtime does **not** mean:

> "Keep the application running forever."

Instead:

```text
iOS decides when execution is available
                │
                ▼
       Background Engine #18
                │
        ┌───────┼────────┐
        ▼       ▼        ▼
       #4      #15      #16
    Network   Storage   Cloud
        │       │        │
        └───────┼────────┘
                ▼
             finish
```

The application requests useful background opportunities from iOS and performs bounded work when granted execution time.

---

# 1. Background Task Types

```swift
import Foundation
import BackgroundTasks
import OSLog
import Observation
import UIKit
```

