# 45. Final Runtime Relationship

```text
                         iOS
                          │
                 ┌────────┴────────┐
                 │                 │
            Scene Sessions      Extensions
                 │                 │
                 ▼                 ▼
              #20 SCENES         #19
                 │
        ┌────────┼─────────┐
        ▼        ▼         ▼
      Scene A  Scene B   Scene C
        │        │         │
        └────────┼─────────┘
                 ▼
             #6 UI/Render
                 │
                 ▼
             #10 MASTER
                 │
       ┌─────────┼─────────┐
       ▼         ▼         ▼
      #3        #18       #15
     Power    Background  Data
       │         │         │
       └─────────┼─────────┘
                 ▼
          Application State
```

## The complete iPhone stack now becomes

```text
#1   Performance Runtime
#2   Memory Management
#3   Battery & Thermal
#4   Networking & Connectivity
#5   Persistent Storage
#6   UI & Rendering
#7   Security & Privacy
#8   On-device Intelligence
#9   Sensor & Hardware
#10  Master Runtime & Diagnostics

#11  Advanced Memory Manager
#12  GPU / Metal Compute
#13  Advanced Camera & Imaging
#14  Media & Audio
#15  Database / Query Engine
#16  Cloud / iCloud
#17  Push Notifications
#18  Background Execution
#19  App Extension Runtime
#20  Multitasking & Scene Manager
```

### Architectural result

**#20 becomes the lifecycle traffic controller for the application UI.**

It knows which scenes exist, their lifecycle state, restoration state, workload and application-level priority. It then communicates those requirements down to **#6 rendering, #3 power/thermal, #4 networking, #8 intelligence, #13 imaging, #14 audio, #18 background execution and #19 extensions**, while leaving iOS itself in control of the actual process, scheduler, compositor, suspension and background-execution decisions.




# iPhone Advanced Connectivity Runtime — Swift

## #21 Advanced Connectivity Runtime

### Architecture

```text
                         iOS NETWORK STACK
                               │
              ┌────────────────┼────────────────┐
              ▼                ▼                ▼
             Wi-Fi          Cellular         Ethernet
              │                │                │
              └────────────────┼────────────────┘
                               ▼
                        NWPathMonitor
                               │
                               ▼
                  #21 CONNECTIVITY RUNTIME
                               │
       ┌───────────────────────┼────────────────────────┐
       ▼                       ▼                        ▼
 Path Intelligence      Local Discovery          Peer Sessions
       │                       │                        │
       ▼                       ▼                        ▼
     #4                    Bonjour                 Multipeer
 Networking                  DNS-SD                Connectivity
       │
       └───────────────────────┬────────────────────────┘
                               ▼
                       Connection Policy
                               │
             ┌─────────────────┼─────────────────┐
             ▼                 ▼                 ▼
          #3 Power           #15 Data           #18 Background
             │                 │                 │
             └─────────────────┼─────────────────┘
                               ▼
                         #10 Master Runtime
```

The distinction is:

```text
#4 = "Can I make this network request?"

#21 = "What connectivity environment am I in,
       what other devices/services are available,
       and which transport should the application use?"
```

---

# 1. Imports

```swift
import Foundation
import Network
import OSLog
import Observation
```

Multipeer Connectivity can be added behind a separate integration boundary:

```swift
import MultipeerConnectivity
```

Keeping it separate prevents peer-to-peer dependencies from contaminating the core networking runtime.

