# 24. Security Policy by Workload

Not every request needs the same attestation cost.

```swift
public enum ProtectedWorkload:
    String,
    Sendable,
    Codable
{
    case publicData
    case accountRead
    case accountWrite
    case financialOperation
    case sensitiveOperation
    case administrativeOperation
}
```

```swift
public actor ProtectedWorkloadPolicy {

    public init() {}

    public func requirement(
        for workload:
            ProtectedWorkload
    )
        -> IntegrityRequirement
    {
        switch workload {

        case .publicData:
            return .none

        case .accountRead:
            return .assertion

        case .accountWrite:
            return .assertion

        case .financialOperation:
            return .attestationAndAssertion

        case .sensitiveOperation:
            return .attestationAndAssertion

        case .administrativeOperation:
            return .attestationAndAssertion
        }
    }
}
```

This is an **application policy example**, not an Apple-mandated classification.

---

# 25. Challenge Lifecycle

```text
Server
  │
  │  cryptographically random challenge
  ▼
iPhone
  │
  ├── App Attest key
  │
  ├── SHA-256 challenge
  │
  └── attestation/assertion
  │
  ▼
#4 HTTPS
  │
  ▼
Server
  │
  ├── verify challenge
  ├── verify app identity
  ├── verify App Attest structure
  ├── verify assertion
  ├── verify counter
  ├── verify request binding
  └── enforce replay protection
```

---

# 26. Replay Protection

A protected request should contain:

```text
request_id
challenge_id
key_id
assertion
body_hash
timestamp
```

The backend should maintain state such as:

```text
key_id
    │
    ├── registered
    ├── last assertion counter
    ├── last challenge
    └── revocation state
```

The client should never be trusted to maintain the authoritative replay state.

---

# 27. Key Rotation

App Attest keys have a lifecycle controlled by Apple's App Attest system.

The application should nevertheless support:

```text
existing key
     │
     ├── usable
     │
     ├── server rejects
     │
     └── key invalid / unavailable
              │
              ▼
        re-registration
```

Do **not** automatically destroy a working key merely because an assertion failed once.

---

# 28. #7 Security Integration

The separation becomes:

```text
#7 SECURITY
│
├── Keychain
├── Secure Enclave
├── LocalAuthentication
├── encryption
├── signing
└── secret management

#22 ATTESTATION
│
├── App Attest
├── DeviceCheck
├── attestation lifecycle
├── assertions
├── challenge lifecycle
└── application integrity
```

#22 can consume #7 but should not duplicate its credential store.

---

# 29. #4 Networking Integration

```text
Application Request
       │
       ▼
#22 Integrity Policy
       │
       ├── no protection
       │
       └── App Attest assertion
                  │
                  ▼
             #4 Network
                  │
                  ▼
                HTTPS
                  │
                  ▼
               Backend
```

The backend remains the authority.

---

# 30. #15 Database Integration

Persist only non-sensitive lifecycle metadata:

```text
attestation_state
key_id
last_attestation
last_assertion
server_registration_state
```

Do not store:

```text
private keys
raw secrets
unnecessary attestation blobs
authentication tokens
```

in ordinary database tables.

---

# 31. #16 Cloud Integration

Attestation should generally protect application/backend operations, not be used as a substitute for CloudKit's own authentication and authorization model.

```text
#22
 │
 ▼
Application Backend
 │
 ▼
#16 Cloud / iCloud
```

Where CloudKit directly handles the operation, use CloudKit's own identity/security mechanisms.

---

# 32. #17 Push Integration

A push notification can initiate an application workflow, but it should not itself be treated as proof of device integrity.

```text
APNs
 │
 ▼
#17 Push
 │
 ▼
Application
 │
 ▼
#22 assertion when sensitive action required
```

---

# 33. #18 Background Integration

Attestation operations can require network/server interaction.

Therefore:

```text
#18 Background
      │
      ▼
#22 Attestation
      │
      ▼
#4 Networking
```

Background execution is still subject to iOS scheduling and execution limits.

---

# 34. #19 Extension Integration

Extensions should not automatically inherit the main application's attestation state.

```text
Extension
    │
    ▼
shared application state
    │
    ▼
Main application
    │
    ▼
#22
```

If an extension needs protected backend access, design an explicit authentication/attestation protocol appropriate to that extension.

Never put private cryptographic material into an App Group shared file.

---

# 35. #20 Scene Integration

Different scenes can share one App Attest key while maintaining independent UI state.

```text
Scene A ─┐
Scene B ─┼──► #22
Scene C ─┘
```

The key belongs to the app installation, not to a particular UI scene.

