# 14. Core ML Runtime

#8 already owns basic inference.

#23 should use a dedicated adapter:

```swift
public protocol CoreMLInferenceProvider:
    Sendable
{
    func infer(
        model:
            String,
        input:
            Data
    )
        async throws
        -> Data
}
```

---

# 15. Core ML Adapter

```swift
public actor CoreMLInferenceAdapter:
    CoreMLInferenceProvider
{
    private var models:
        [String: MLModel] = [:]

    public init() {}

    public func load(
        identifier:
            String,
        url:
            URL,
        configuration:
            MLModelConfiguration =
                MLModelConfiguration()
    )
        throws
    {
        let model =
            try MLModel(
                contentsOf:
                    url,
                configuration:
                    configuration
            )

        models[
            identifier
        ] =
            model
    }

    public func infer(
        model:
            String,
        input:
            Data
    )
        async throws
        -> Data
    {
        guard
            models[model] != nil
        else {
            throw AIRuntimeError
                .modelNotFound
        }

        // Actual feature conversion belongs
        // to the concrete model adapter.
        //
        // Different Core ML models have different
        // MLFeatureProvider contracts.

        throw AIRuntimeError
            .invalidRequest
    }
}
```

The intentionally abstract input boundary prevents pretending that every Core ML model accepts raw `Data`.

Core ML allows applications to configure compute-unit usage and preferred Metal devices through `MLModelConfiguration`.

