# 49. Production File Architecture

The completed stack now becomes:

```text
                           iOS APPLICATION
                                │
                                ▼
                         #10 MASTER RUNTIME
                                │
      ┌─────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────┐
      │                         │                         │
      ▼                         ▼                         ▼
   #15 DATA                  #26 FILES                 #16 CLOUD
   DATABASE                     │                         │
      │                         │                         │
      │              ┌──────────┼──────────┐              │
      │              ▼          ▼          ▼              │
      │           IMPORT     EXPORT     DOCUMENTS         │
      │              │          │          │              │
      └──────────────┴──────────┼──────────┴──────────────┘
                                ▼
                              #25
                            SEARCH
                                │
                                ▼
                              #6 UI
```

---

# 50. Core Rules

The production version of #26 should follow these rules:

```text
1. Never expose arbitrary filesystem access.
2. Validate relative paths.
3. Keep filesystem objects behind actor boundaries.
4. Use atomic writes for important application files.
5. Use streaming for large files.
6. Keep authoritative metadata in #15.
7. Treat search indexes as rebuildable.
8. Use security-scoped access only when required.
9. Balance security-scoped resource access.
10. Respect iCloud/App Group entitlements.
11. Do not assume cloud availability.
12. Do not put secrets into ordinary files.
13. Keep extension data compact.
14. Make expensive indexing cancellable.
15. Integrate power and memory budgets.
16. Preserve file identity independently from paths.
17. Check content types using public Uniform Type Identifier APIs.
18. Keep document-provider/UI APIs on the appropriate MainActor boundary.
```

---

# 51. #26 → #27

The next layer should be:

```text
#27 ENERGY BUDGET & RESOURCE ACCOUNTING RUNTIME
```

It can sit above #3 Power/Thermal and provide the entire stack with explicit application-level budgets:

```text
                     #27 ENERGY BUDGET
                            │
             ┌──────────────┼──────────────┐
             ▼              ▼              ▼
           CPU             GPU             AI
             │              │              │
           #1/#23          #12            #23
             │              │              │
             └──────────────┼──────────────┘
                            ▼
                         #3 POWER
                            │
                            ▼
                       RUN / DEFER
```

That would give the architecture a proper **resource-accounting layer**, rather than having each subsystem independently make power decisions.



# iPhone #27 — Energy Budget & Resource Accounting Runtime — Swift

## 1. Architecture

```text
                         #10 MASTER RUNTIME
                                │
                                ▼
                  ┌──────────────────────────┐
                  │ #27 ENERGY / RESOURCE    │
                  │       GOVERNOR           │
                  └────────────┬─────────────┘
                               │
       ┌───────────────┬───────┼────────┬──────────────┐
       ▼               ▼       ▼        ▼              ▼
      CPU             GPU      AI      NETWORK         I/O
       │               │       │        │              │
      #1              #12     #23      #4             #15/#26
       │               │       │        │              │
       └───────────────┴───────┼────────┴──────────────┘
                               ▼
                         #3 POWER/THERMAL
                               │
                               ▼
                     RUN / DEFER / THROTTLE
```

#27 is deliberately an **application policy layer**.

It cannot directly set CPU frequency, GPU frequency, battery charging behaviour, thermal hardware limits, or Neural Engine frequency.

