The next logical layer is **#28 Thermal Prediction & Adaptive Workload Forecasting**, which can use the telemetry from #3 and accounting from #27 to estimate when sustained workloads are likely to become thermally constrained and proactively change workload policy.




# iPhone #28 — Thermal Prediction & Adaptive Workload Forecasting Runtime — Swift

## 1. Purpose

```text
                    #10 MASTER RUNTIME
                           │
                           ▼
                  ┌──────────────────┐
                  │ #28 THERMAL      │
                  │ PREDICTION       │
                  │ & FORECASTING    │
                  └────────┬─────────┘
                           │
             ┌─────────────┼─────────────┐
             ▼             ▼             ▼
            #3            #27            #1
       Power/Thermal   Energy Budget  Performance
             │             │             │
             └─────────────┼─────────────┘
                           ▼
                  WORKLOAD FORECAST
                           │
             ┌─────────────┼─────────────┐
             ▼             ▼             ▼
          MAINTAIN      THROTTLE       DEFER
```

#28 does **not** attempt to read private thermal sensors or predict Apple's internal hardware temperature.

It forecasts application-level thermal pressure using public, observable signals such as:

* `ProcessInfo.ThermalState`
* Low Power Mode
* workload duration
* CPU/GPU/AI workload accounting from #27
* memory pressure
* network/disk activity
* application lifecycle state
* historical workload behaviour

The output is a **policy forecast**, not a hardware temperature prediction.

